WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.230
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Hello,
and welcome to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.420
our live webinars
from Google Cloud that

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.680
happen every Tuesday.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.740
Today we are talking
about the future

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.010
of databases in the cloud and
my name is Deepti Srivastava.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.980
I am the product manager
for Cloud Spanner.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.510
So let's get started.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:38.780
So before we talk
about Spanner and why

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.930
we think it's the future
of databases and cloud,

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:45.440
I think it's important to go
through a little bit of how

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:46.820
we got here, all of us.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:55.730
How IT, and databases,
and data itself got here.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:01.340
So for cloud, it is
still very, very early.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:07.160
So most of global IT spend, as
you can see here from Gartner,

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.160
most of the global IT
spend is still on prem

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.430
and very little of it, very
small percentage of it,

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:15.800
is actually in the cloud so far.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.010
So most of cloud growth
is going to happen

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:20.540
in the next 5 to 10 years.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:30.290
Welcome back.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:31.504
Apologies for that.

00:02:31.504 --> 00:02:32.920
We've fixed the
issue on our side,

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.630
so we'll just continue
right where we were.

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.380
And we were very early
in our presentation

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.880
too, so you haven't
lost anything

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.151
if you're just tuning in.

00:02:41.151 --> 00:02:41.650
All right.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:45.490
So we were talking about global
IT spend and how much of it

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:46.840
is really in cloud.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.550
And as you can see
from this chart

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:53.050
that we've got from Gartner
here, from last year's Gartner

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:58.630
reports, very little of
it is still in cloud.

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:04.720
So each of these businesses and
each of these infrastructures

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.040
have a database.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.170
You always need
to have a database

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:11.260
to store your critical data
or not so critical data.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:17.200
So for database spend, it's
extremely early in terms

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:18.340
of clouded option.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:24.280
So as you can see here, first
of all, most of database spend

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:30.820
is in operational DBMSs
and very little of it

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:34.990
is actually in the
non-relational open source

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:36.160
world.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.560
Despite all the
marketing you hear

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.070
from the non-relational
side of the spectrum here,

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:46.300
most data is actually stored
in relational databases.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.600
And that's obvious, right?

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.760
Because with relational
databases you get transactions.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.790
You get atomicity of updates.

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:56.710
You get integrity of data.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:59.560
You get consistency
guarantees, et cetera.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.650
Which are, of course, very,
very useful, as we all know,

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:06.890
for developing applications.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:16.010
So obviously this implies
that a lot of the databases

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:19.040
out there are
relational and most

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.280
of the mission critical
data that a company has

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:24.980
will be stored in
relational databases.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:29.804
So before we jump into the
growth of relational databases,

00:04:29.804 --> 00:04:31.970
it's important to talk about
what we mean by mission

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:32.590
critical.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.923
So when we are talking about
mission critical data, what

00:04:34.923 --> 00:04:39.560
we really mean is data
that is business critical.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:43.220
So loss of that data or even
a glitch in serving that data

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:50.700
affects your reputation and has
financial risk to your company.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.650
So all of this data
that is required

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:56.930
for you to run your
business is what

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:58.670
we call mission
critical data, and we're

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:02.600
saying that most of that mission
critical data is stored in most

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.330
cases in relational databases.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:11.730
So again, where are we at with
the current state of databases?

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:15.930
So most companies, as we talked
about earlier, most of the IT

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:16.910
spend is still on prem.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:19.430
What that means is that
most databases are still

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:20.270
running on prem.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:22.610
And most companies
are then managing

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:25.760
their own inflexible
database infrastructure

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:28.070
on premises with
an army of people

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:31.160
trying to maintain
that infrastructure,

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.370
and maintain their databases,
and keep them alive

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:35.060
and running.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:40.220
And of course, there is a
huge overhead in having those.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.130
There are those companies
who have taken the leap

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:45.820
and adopted cloud
to some extent.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:49.040
And what that means is they
are using infrastructure

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.250
as a service from
cloud providers.

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:55.520
So they're running their
own database solutions

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.340
on top of these cloud
provider infrastructure.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.860
So they're still in the business
of maintaining, managing,

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.220
and administering, and
keeping their databases alive.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.532
They may not need to run
the machines themselves

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:08.240
that host those
databases, but they still

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.240
have to deal with the database
maintenance and management.

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:14.510
And then there are companies who
have taken a bigger leap where

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:16.430
they're actually
paying a service

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.890
provider to manage
and run their database

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:20.340
solutions in the cloud.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:21.980
So this means that
they don't have

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:25.300
to worry about actually
keeping the database alive.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.810
There is a service
that's doing that.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.620
But that still
means that they have

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:33.710
to make sure that the
databases are running

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:35.390
the way that's expected.

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:37.580
They have to deal
with the service

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:39.530
provider for their
database service

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:43.370
and maybe deal with other
providers for other services.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:45.400
So there is some
complication there.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:50.330
Now apart from where the systems
and the databases are running,

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:53.960
the other limitation is
in technology itself.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:56.660
So companies are running
into limitations around RDBMS

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:59.250
scale up, and scale out,
and sharding solutions.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:02.300
So if you run out of capacity of
your database, what do you do?

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:04.670
You have to either scale
up to bigger machines

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:06.590
or you have to go to
the sharded solutions.

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:08.256
Then they come with
their own headaches,

00:07:08.256 --> 00:07:10.670
which we'll get into
in a little bit.

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:15.500
And finally, companies are
dealing with the administration

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.670
and consistency challenges
of scalable solutions

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:22.940
that are also NoSQL
or non-relational.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:24.980
So for those
companies who already

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:28.370
hit the problem
with their databases

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:31.670
and the scale of them, and
have adopted more scalable

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:35.690
solutions that are
NoSQL or non-relational,

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:39.230
they're trading off
consistency for scale

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:41.820
or the traditional relational
semantics for scale.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:45.230
They're running into challenges
of having the consistency

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:47.570
issues and the challenges
in running and administering

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:51.000
these NoSQL solutions, whether
on prem or in the cloud.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.740
The technology
challenges still remain.

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:59.630
So what does that mean
for where we are in terms

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:03.170
of the spectrum of technology?

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:06.800
So we have this very
interesting tradeoff

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.470
between traditional
relational databases

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:10.520
and traditional
non-relational databases.

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:12.590
So on the business side,
traditional databases

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:17.240
provide you with schema,
with SQL, with consistency.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:19.820
And the NoSQL or
non-traditional solutions

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:21.290
don't provide you
those, but they

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:23.330
trade those off for
high availability,

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:27.980
horizontal scalability,
replication, et cetera.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:31.190
Which is the exact limitations
of traditional relational

00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:32.539
databases, in most cases.

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:39.890
What that means is, when you
want to run high availability

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:44.059
relational databases for your
mission critical applications,

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:48.800
you have to create these
highly complex architectures

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:53.060
to provide that availability,
and replication, and disaster

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:57.300
recovery solutions for
your mission critical data.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:00.860
And, as you can see, you're
running multiple masters,

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:03.410
you're running read
replicas for scaling out

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:08.860
and offloading the read-only
data access, et cetera.

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:11.410
You're trying to have memcache
in there somewhere so that you

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:15.670
can do faster reads
of your data, which

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:18.340
means you have to deal with
the flushing of the cache.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:21.550
And you have to deal
with consistency issues

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:23.980
with having multiple
masters, if you need that.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:26.060
You have backup replicas.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:28.960
So managing this
system, now you've

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:31.780
gone from one database
that hosts your mission

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:36.160
critical data to this
complicated architecture that,

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:38.050
again, requires an
army to maintain.

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:39.940
You don't actually know
where the bottlenecks

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:42.670
are, in some cases, because
the system is so fragile.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:47.129
And god forbid if one
piece of it somehow fails

00:09:47.129 --> 00:09:49.670
and then you have to figure out
what the cascade effects are.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:51.250
And in most cases,
you don't actually

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:53.530
even know what the
full picture is

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:56.660
because it's become such a
complicated organically grown

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:57.160
beast.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:04.960
So going back to
first principles,

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:06.190
what do customers want?

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:08.620
What do you want
from your database?

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:13.120
So obviously, you want it to be
highly available and scalable.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:15.040
And you want it to give
you data consistency

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:18.370
so you can access
data confidently

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:19.450
for your application.

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:22.510
And you can serve the
correct inaccurate data

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:24.280
to your customers.

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:27.100
And of course, from a
developer standpoint,

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:29.870
when you're building and
using these databases,

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:31.420
you want simplified operations.

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:33.310
You want standards.

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:36.404
You want single points
of contact for support.

00:10:36.404 --> 00:10:38.320
You don't want to have
to talk to the database

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:42.640
provider, and the infrastructure
manager, and the cloud provider

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:43.570
separately.

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:47.110
You want to have one
person or one contact point

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:49.970
that you can go to if
you have any issues.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:53.020
So you don't have
to deal with crises

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:57.670
by being the coordinator of
various pieces in that crisis.

00:10:57.670 --> 00:10:59.650
Basically you want
your databases

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:01.960
to run while you
can build and grow

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:04.460
your application
and your business.

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:07.540
And this is where
Spanner comes in.

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:12.160
So why do we build Spanner?

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:15.770
And how did we get here?

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:18.940
So if we go back
in time to 2005,

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:21.310
technical
infrastructure at Google

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:24.910
had various scalable
solutions because we'd already

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:28.000
reached the scale versus
consistency problem.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:32.380
So we had scalable
data storage systems.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:36.640
But we were lacking
in transactions

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:40.270
and relational semantics
in those systems.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:42.580
And as we've learned,
as we all know,

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:45.760
those little semantics, SQL,
et cetera, are very important.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:50.950
And they really help you
run your business well.

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:53.650
So Google reached that point.

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:58.750
And so the need of the hour was
horizontally scaling databases.

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:01.570
We have ACID transactions
and global consistency.

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:04.780
And of course, we are Google,
so we want no downtime.

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:06.156
We want high availability.

00:12:06.156 --> 00:12:07.780
We want you to be
able to access Google

00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:09.560
from anywhere at all times.

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:11.490
So we came up with Spanner.

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.780
We developed Spanner
with a lot of research

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.390
and a lot of
understanding of how

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:21.910
to build distributed, scalable,
highly available systems.

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:25.240
And what we have here is the
paper that we published in 2012

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:29.420
that talks about the technology
that we built at the time.

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:33.370
So Spanner was
launched externally

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:37.840
as Cloud Spanner, which is
a publicly available product

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:40.330
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:42.310
And it's about
exactly a year ago

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:45.560
that it was launched
publicly on GCP.

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:48.600
So we're celebrating
our first anniversary

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:51.550
as a cloud database.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:55.660
So Cloud Spanner is
an enterprise-grade

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:58.550
globally-distributed and
strongly-consistent database

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:00.880
service built
specifically for the cloud

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:06.460
to combine the benefits of our
relational database structure

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:09.620
with non-relational
horizontal scale.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:11.699
So what does that mean?

00:13:11.699 --> 00:13:13.240
We're basically
saying you don't have

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:17.590
to trade off or make compromises
between relational semantics

00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:21.730
and horizontal scale, which is
what traditionally companies

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:25.690
and IT folks have had to do.

00:13:25.690 --> 00:13:28.000
Database users have had to do.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:30.790
They've had to basically
trade off and give up

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:34.330
on relational semantics and
build that in the application

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:38.020
to get horizontal scale
to manage their data

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:42.940
growth over the course
of the data exploding

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:45.040
for their business.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:49.510
So Spanner allows you to not
have to make that compromise.

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:53.020
It's our no compromise
relational database service.

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:56.090
And you get all of this packaged
up in a managed service,

00:13:56.090 --> 00:14:02.530
so you can focus on the
application using that database

00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:05.330
and not have to worry about
running the database itself.

00:14:05.330 --> 00:14:09.790
So we provide schemas,
ACID transactions, SQL,

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:13.330
and the relational scale
with up to five lines

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:15.720
of availability and
an availability SLA,

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:17.310
so we guarantee that.

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:21.620
We're in a fully managed,
easily scalable service.

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:25.180
So if we hark back
to our chart of what

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:29.890
are the pros and
cons of relational

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:33.100
versus traditional
non-relational services

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:37.120
or databases, you don't have
to make those tradeoffs.

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:39.220
We have schemas, we have
SQL, we have consistency,

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:41.140
like traditional
relational databases.

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:43.780
And we have availability,
scale, and replication.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:48.100
And, by the way, a bunch of
it out of the box that you

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:55.270
can get with non-relational
traditional NoSQL systems.

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:58.150
So again, you don't
have to make compromises

00:14:58.150 --> 00:15:01.900
between technical needs
and business needs

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:04.360
in order to really
run your applications

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:05.830
and your business critical data.

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:13.570
Now there are differences
in the technology of Spanner

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:15.070
and traditional
relational databases

00:15:15.070 --> 00:15:18.370
and how they've worked so far.

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:21.010
And of course, there
is innovation here

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:23.950
that allows us to do this
relational with scale.

00:15:23.950 --> 00:15:25.700
And I can touch upon
a little bit of that.

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:27.366
But the important
thing to note here is,

00:15:27.366 --> 00:15:30.790
like traditional databases,
you get tables, you get DDL,

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:34.450
you get the same schemas
that you're used to.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:38.170
So you do create table, you
have primary keys, et cetera.

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:40.930
And in this picture,
you can see that we've

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:45.280
created two different tables
which have relationships

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:46.960
that you can see.

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:49.540
And you use simple
DDL statements

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:51.070
in order to create this table.

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:57.730
And you'll use this schema
change DDL statements as well

00:15:57.730 --> 00:16:00.500
to alter table or drop
column, et cetera, as well.

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:04.360
So again, these are
the familiar constructs

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:07.250
that you're used to so far
with traditional relational

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:13.030
that you get with Spanner as
well with some added benefits.

00:16:13.030 --> 00:16:16.550
So in this case, you
see these two tables.

00:16:16.550 --> 00:16:22.470
And if we were to see how this
logical structure is laid out,

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:24.140
Spanner has a concept
of interleaving.

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:25.990
It's one of the many
technical differences,

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:27.370
but it's interesting
to point out

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:30.250
as it showcases how
we've been built

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:35.032
for scale with the relational
transactional semantics.

00:16:35.032 --> 00:16:37.240
So in the previous case, we
had two different tables.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:38.573
And they're logically different.

00:16:38.573 --> 00:16:41.060
There's the albums table
and the singers table.

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:44.230
But if you interleave the albums
table and the singers table,

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:48.490
what that means is that the
data is logically co-located.

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:51.310
So you essentially can
think of it as a pre-join.

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:56.350
So when you're doing large
data set reads or scans,

00:16:56.350 --> 00:17:01.930
you don't have to actually
do the join at a run time.

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:04.780
You can predetermine
that these data sets are

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:09.280
going to be queried together
and you can logically

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:12.460
tell the system to store
them together so that they

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:16.134
can be retrieved much faster.

00:17:16.134 --> 00:17:18.880
For a few MBs or
a few GBs of data,

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:23.569
joining may not be a
latency causing issue.

00:17:23.569 --> 00:17:26.566
But when you have hundreds
of gigabytes or hundreds

00:17:26.566 --> 00:17:27.940
of terabytes of
data and you need

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:32.980
to do all albums for
all singers, a query,

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:35.680
that's a lot of data to
be joined and processed

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:38.180
at runtime, at query time.

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:40.960
So instead of having to
do a join at that time,

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:42.760
you can specify to
this system that you

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:45.280
need to pre-join this
data set because your most

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:49.150
common queries are actually
going to scan the singers along

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:50.410
with their albums.

00:17:50.410 --> 00:17:56.410
So in this case, the system
can very quickly read the data

00:17:56.410 --> 00:17:58.300
and return the query
results without having

00:17:58.300 --> 00:18:00.800
to do the computationally
intensive join,

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:07.850
hence reducing read time
or query serving time.

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:12.850
Again, you can use interleaving
for indexes as well.

00:18:12.850 --> 00:18:14.080
So we have secondary indexes.

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:15.340
We are a relational database.

00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:18.430
And from that standpoint,
you can, of course,

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:22.780
have separate logical
indexes, which

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:27.657
can be standard tables in
other traditional databases.

00:18:27.657 --> 00:18:29.740
And in our case, they are
standard tables as well,

00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:31.450
but you can interleave them.

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:34.540
So again, if you're going
to have to back join indexes

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:39.070
with the base table because your
most common query is requiring

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:42.190
that pattern, then you
can interleave the indexes

00:18:42.190 --> 00:18:45.250
within the base table.

00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:48.910
And again, that can lead to
much faster selects and queries

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:50.510
of your data.

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:54.250
So this is just one of the ways
in which Spanner is different.

00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:57.010
But it shows you
under the covers

00:18:57.010 --> 00:18:58.990
the technological
differences we have,

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:04.660
which allows us to provide
scale with relational semantics.

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:12.640
So, as Nick says in
this quote from Gartner,

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:14.920
when we launched
Spanner, and we talked

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:17.980
about it with various
analysts, and of course we

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:20.900
have customers
that are using it,

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:23.200
it's interesting
because it's not just

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:25.090
another take on a
traditional database.

00:19:25.090 --> 00:19:27.560
It's really a new
way of thinking,

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:32.230
a new need that's being met by
the technologies at Spanner.

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:34.430
But Spanner is
not just software.

00:19:34.430 --> 00:19:40.600
It's the combination of a lot
of very clever software, which

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:44.140
there is a lot of, along
with hardware that we built

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:50.560
specifically for scale and then
to provide the kinds of things

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:52.900
that we need for a large,
highly available, scalable

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:57.130
system, such as atomic
clocks that we have in-house.

00:19:57.130 --> 00:20:00.370
TrueTime is a technology we
use for global synchronization

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:02.680
and external consistency.

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:06.490
Which is stronger
than linearizability.

00:20:06.490 --> 00:20:10.570
So we use TrueTime, which is
based on atomic clocks and GPS,

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:12.580
to do this coordination.

00:20:12.580 --> 00:20:16.000
And that powers
Cloud Spanner as well

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:18.830
and allows it to have that
global external consistency,

00:20:18.830 --> 00:20:21.490
which allows us to
then have globally

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:26.450
consistent and strong
relational semantics

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:29.290
database in Cloud Spanner.

00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:32.590
And this is, of course, built
on top of our Google network,

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:35.660
which we've talked
about quite a bunch.

00:20:35.660 --> 00:20:37.960
And there is a lot
of white papers

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:40.000
and we've actually put
online as well that one

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:42.070
can read if interested.

00:20:42.070 --> 00:20:43.810
But basically,
it's a combination

00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:46.840
of our Google
networking, which is

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:51.100
highly redundant and
optimized on all levels,

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.770
from the network fabric all
the way to the switches,

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:56.710
to the hubs, et cetera.

00:20:56.710 --> 00:20:59.950
And atomic clocks and
GPS with TrueTime,

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:03.490
as well as clever software
in Cloud Spanner itself.

00:21:03.490 --> 00:21:06.490
So we, for example, have
wrote our own implementation

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:07.510
of Paxos.

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:10.010
We don't use open source
Paxos implementation.

00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:14.320
We optimized the Paxos
algorithm in our implementation

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:16.750
for our specific use case.

00:21:16.750 --> 00:21:20.020
Which allows us to, again,
do low latency transactions

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:22.930
and to do strong reads
across continents

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:25.150
and across oceanic links.

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:29.235
And all of this combines
to give you Cloud Spanner.

00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:37.480
So there's a bunch of customers
that are using Spanner

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:38.860
and they're excited about it.

00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:42.970
And I am personally excited
to talk about a few of them

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:45.830
that I've been seeing them win.

00:21:45.830 --> 00:21:49.270
And it's really
exciting for us to see

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:53.260
how the world has adopted
and started to use Spanner.

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:57.610
So it's not just
about trading off

00:21:57.610 --> 00:22:00.380
CapEx for OpEx, which is what a
managed database service gives

00:22:00.380 --> 00:22:00.880
you.

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:02.470
That's kind of the
promise of cloud.

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:04.330
But with cloud,
customers are trying

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:07.452
to do inherently new
and exciting things

00:22:07.452 --> 00:22:09.160
that they couldn't do
before because they

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:10.850
were limited by technology.

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:15.190
And in this case, Optiva, which
is a telecom communications

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:20.350
billing and charging system, as
mission critical as they get,

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:26.380
it has adopted Spanner as its
next generation database system

00:22:26.380 --> 00:22:30.040
for its next
generations of software

00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:32.740
for billing and charging.

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:36.130
And when they looked
at Spanner, they

00:22:36.130 --> 00:22:40.450
saw improved performance and
reliability from their existing

00:22:40.450 --> 00:22:42.040
traditional architectures.

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:44.800
And they actually saw 10
times more throughput,

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:47.270
so 10 times more
transactions per second,

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:49.390
at a dramatically reduced cost.

00:22:49.390 --> 00:22:53.110
So Optiva was actually
presenting this week

00:22:53.110 --> 00:22:55.954
at Mobile World
Congress in Barcelona.

00:22:55.954 --> 00:22:57.620
And I'm here with you
and I'm not there.

00:22:57.620 --> 00:23:02.170
But it's really exciting
because they're really

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:03.670
excited about the technology.

00:23:03.670 --> 00:23:06.160
They're really excited
about the possibilities

00:23:06.160 --> 00:23:09.670
it opens up for them
and for their customers.

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:14.230
And that's really the reason to
have Cloud Spanner as a managed

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:14.980
database service.

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:17.770
They don't have to worry
about the system itself.

00:23:17.770 --> 00:23:19.450
And also, because
of its combination

00:23:19.450 --> 00:23:22.880
of relational semantics
and horizontal scale,

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:25.060
they can do things
with this technology

00:23:25.060 --> 00:23:29.230
that they couldn't do with their
existing on prem solutions.

00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:33.880
So another interesting example,
one of my favorites actually,

00:23:33.880 --> 00:23:36.640
Marketo and the one
following, is again,

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:41.470
Marketo is a marketing
platform that

00:23:41.470 --> 00:23:45.430
provides marketing
applications for companies

00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:46.870
all over the world.

00:23:46.870 --> 00:23:49.480
And for them, in
their own words,

00:23:49.480 --> 00:23:53.680
having order sequence in
their application logic

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:55.120
is very important.

00:23:55.120 --> 00:24:00.430
And, as they were looking
to go to cloud and looking,

00:24:00.430 --> 00:24:03.730
obviously, the first step is,
which cloud provider do I use?

00:24:03.730 --> 00:24:06.200
And the second
question for most of us

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:10.010
is, what database services do
each of these cloud providers

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:10.880
have?

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:12.840
And how do I use them?

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:15.320
Is it easier for me
to use these services

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:18.620
or is it going to make
my life more difficult?

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:22.850
And the Cloud
Spanner, again, they

00:24:22.850 --> 00:24:26.930
could use the system
to have the order,

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:29.120
and sequence guarantees,
and the app logic,

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:31.820
so they didn't have to
write brittle application

00:24:31.820 --> 00:24:33.890
logic to have that sequencing.

00:24:33.890 --> 00:24:38.590
With NoSQL and with
systems that are scaled,

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:41.150
but lacking in
transactional consistency

00:24:41.150 --> 00:24:43.130
or strong consistency,
all of that

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:44.570
has to be built into the app.

00:24:44.570 --> 00:24:47.330
Which means your application
becomes a lot more complicated

00:24:47.330 --> 00:24:50.870
and your team is
basically stressing

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:53.330
in keeping that system
alive and running

00:24:53.330 --> 00:24:58.340
instead of building cool
things to serve your customers.

00:24:58.340 --> 00:25:02.060
And that obviously is a
productivity hit, but also

00:25:02.060 --> 00:25:03.380
a growth hit for you.

00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:05.229
It affects your
business bottom line.

00:25:05.229 --> 00:25:06.770
And in this case,
when you don't have

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:09.557
to worry about that anymore,
you can choose Spanner.

00:25:09.557 --> 00:25:12.140
You don't have to re-platform
or think of re-platforming ever.

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:15.020
You just grow as your
global business grows.

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:16.430
That's a powerful statement.

00:25:18.950 --> 00:25:23.540
And another one of our
favorite customers, Evernote,

00:25:23.540 --> 00:25:26.300
I think a lot of
people know Evernote.

00:25:26.300 --> 00:25:30.950
Again, they had a multiple
hundred shard SQL system.

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:34.490
They want their users' notes
to be obviously available,

00:25:34.490 --> 00:25:36.290
no matter where they are.

00:25:36.290 --> 00:25:37.940
And they.

00:25:37.940 --> 00:25:42.230
With Cloud Spanner,
can go to a system that

00:25:42.230 --> 00:25:44.600
reduces this complexity,
gives them scale.

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:46.220
And once they have
migrated, they

00:25:46.220 --> 00:25:49.690
don't actually have to think
about the re-platforming

00:25:49.690 --> 00:25:50.480
ever again.

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:53.990
So it reduces, in fact,
eliminates, all of that

00:25:53.990 --> 00:26:00.650
stress of managing the
complicated multi-shard mySQL

00:26:00.650 --> 00:26:05.450
system that keeps their
operations team awake at night.

00:26:05.450 --> 00:26:09.860
From that to building
more functionality

00:26:09.860 --> 00:26:17.250
and having ease of use of their
application and infrastructure.

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:24.060
So we talked about the positive
things in Cloud Spanner

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:26.690
or how it's different from
traditional relational

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:27.760
non-relational databases.

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:30.180
But it's important to note
that Google Cloud platform's

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:32.010
database portfolio is vast.

00:26:32.010 --> 00:26:34.550
And Spanner is one piece of it.

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:40.950
We actually have memcache all
the way on the left to our data

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:42.760
warehouse in the
cloud with BigQuery.

00:26:42.760 --> 00:26:46.170
So the way I think about
Cloud Spanner and BigQuery

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:50.160
is, if you're used to
traditional relational

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:55.650
databases and data warehouses,
Cloud Spanner and BigQuery are

00:26:55.650 --> 00:26:58.230
the cloud equivalents of those.

00:26:58.230 --> 00:27:02.610
But Cloud Spanner is
deliberately placed

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:05.280
next to non-relational
systems in this chart

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:09.840
because it allows you to
have non-relational scale,

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:12.330
along with the relational
semantics that it offers.

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:17.550
So the whole portfolio allows
you to have a bunch of tools

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:21.120
that you can choose from to
build the kinds of systems

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:22.920
you need for your application.

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:26.790
We're not trying to tie you down
to one and only one solution,

00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:29.469
but we're not giving
you 100s to choose from.

00:27:29.469 --> 00:27:31.260
We think there are
different use cases that

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:35.040
require very specific,
tailored solutions for storage.

00:27:35.040 --> 00:27:37.710
And with this portfolio
we basically offer

00:27:37.710 --> 00:27:40.320
you a solution for each of
those specific use cases.

00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:46.850
What that also
means is, again, you

00:27:46.850 --> 00:27:50.690
have the toolkit to
choose the right system.

00:27:50.690 --> 00:27:55.550
But you're not left on
your own to figure out

00:27:55.550 --> 00:28:00.230
which system you need to use
among hundreds or dozens.

00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:04.400
There is, as I said, a solution
for each specific use case

00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:09.270
and we hope you choose the
right system for the use case.

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:14.030
So as this chart shows,
there is a flow chart

00:28:14.030 --> 00:28:16.340
that you can use to
pick the system you

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:18.710
want for the use case you have.

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:23.060
And this is, again, up
on our docs in cloud,

00:28:23.060 --> 00:28:25.650
so you can look it
up there as well,

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:28.620
along with a lot
more information.

00:28:28.620 --> 00:28:33.320
So hopefully that
gave you an overview

00:28:33.320 --> 00:28:36.830
of why we think of Cloud Spanner
as the future of database

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:37.957
systems.

00:28:37.957 --> 00:28:40.040
And we'll take a short
break of less than a minute

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:43.550
and come back for a live Q&amp;A
section as well, so stay tuned.

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:23.720
Welcome back to Cloud OnAir.

00:29:23.720 --> 00:29:26.810
Let's get straight to
our audience questions

00:29:26.810 --> 00:29:29.840
for the day from this webinar.

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:35.270
So question 1, are you expecting
all relational databases

00:29:35.270 --> 00:29:36.890
to migrate to cloud immediately?

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:38.480
Absolutely not.

00:29:38.480 --> 00:29:46.010
There are many reasons
why most of the IT

00:29:46.010 --> 00:29:48.620
spend is still on prem
and not in the cloud.

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:51.290
As I had mentioned
earlier in my talk,

00:29:51.290 --> 00:29:54.140
we know that these are
early days of cloud.

00:29:54.140 --> 00:29:57.230
But what we want to make sure
is that customers understand

00:29:57.230 --> 00:30:01.105
that they don't need to shy
away from adopting cloud

00:30:01.105 --> 00:30:02.480
because they think
they can't get

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:04.580
the performance or the
semantics that they're

00:30:04.580 --> 00:30:07.970
expecting from their
applications in the cloud.

00:30:07.970 --> 00:30:12.320
And if you're needing horizontal
scale and relational semantics,

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:16.400
for example, you
don't have to worry

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:18.920
about not being able
to do that in the cloud

00:30:18.920 --> 00:30:20.895
because, for example,
we have Cloud Spanner

00:30:20.895 --> 00:30:23.180
on Google Cloud that will
allow you to have that

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:25.590
without compromises.

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:29.480
So we expect most of
the cloud migration

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:33.999
to happen in the next three
to five years and beyond.

00:30:33.999 --> 00:30:36.290
There are many reasons for
not being in the cloud right

00:30:36.290 --> 00:30:37.280
now, as I said.

00:30:37.280 --> 00:30:39.238
You've already made
commitments, you've already

00:30:39.238 --> 00:30:41.780
bought the machines, you've
already got data center space,

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:45.470
you've already got colo space
for your data infrastructure.

00:30:45.470 --> 00:30:46.880
So it'll take time.

00:30:46.880 --> 00:30:49.850
But I know that a
lot of companies

00:30:49.850 --> 00:30:51.890
are looking at
migrating to the cloud

00:30:51.890 --> 00:30:54.662
and it's important that they
see the full picture and all

00:30:54.662 --> 00:30:56.870
the options available to
them so that they don't have

00:30:56.870 --> 00:30:58.490
to shy away from
it for, let's say,

00:30:58.490 --> 00:31:02.760
performance, or manageability,
or any of those reasons.

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:04.850
So moving on.

00:31:04.850 --> 00:31:06.920
Why not just use a
non-relational database

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:09.130
on prem or in the cloud?

00:31:09.130 --> 00:31:13.010
So I think I touched
upon that earlier.

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:15.170
If you want to use a
non-relational database,

00:31:15.170 --> 00:31:17.070
that's totally up to you.

00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:19.520
However, non-relational
databases

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:23.060
come with the complexity
that we talked about.

00:31:23.060 --> 00:31:24.800
If you need
relational semantics,

00:31:24.800 --> 00:31:28.190
and most mission critical
data does, like bank systems,

00:31:28.190 --> 00:31:31.604
telco systems,
health care systems.

00:31:31.604 --> 00:31:33.020
When you need
relational semantics

00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:35.060
and you're trying to use a
non-relational database, what

00:31:35.060 --> 00:31:37.518
that means is you've given up
on transaction of the system,

00:31:37.518 --> 00:31:39.170
you've given up on
strong consistency,

00:31:39.170 --> 00:31:42.740
you've given on structured
data, you've given up on SQL.

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:44.630
SQL-like is not the
same as SQL, right?

00:31:44.630 --> 00:31:46.700
Anybody who's used
a SQL-like system

00:31:46.700 --> 00:31:48.560
can know that it's not SQL.

00:31:48.560 --> 00:31:50.630
So you've given
up on all of those

00:31:50.630 --> 00:31:53.390
to get the scale with these
non-relational databases.

00:31:53.390 --> 00:31:55.500
And if that works
for you, great.

00:31:55.500 --> 00:31:59.021
But it does mean that if
you need those things,

00:31:59.021 --> 00:32:00.770
you have to build them
in the application.

00:32:00.770 --> 00:32:02.686
And anybody who's even
attempted to build them

00:32:02.686 --> 00:32:05.510
knows that this is
super complicated code.

00:32:05.510 --> 00:32:09.530
It's got so many
edge cases that you

00:32:09.530 --> 00:32:12.860
have to think about
and take care of.

00:32:12.860 --> 00:32:16.850
It's brittle because actually
rolling your own transaction

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:19.040
in the application
layer is hard.

00:32:19.040 --> 00:32:22.850
[INAUDIBLE] in the sharded
solution, for example.

00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:26.660
Doing the logic to
join between two shards

00:32:26.660 --> 00:32:29.340
and all those kinds of
things are really hard.

00:32:29.340 --> 00:32:31.340
And again, if you like
doing that kind of stuff,

00:32:31.340 --> 00:32:31.820
that's great.

00:32:31.820 --> 00:32:33.230
But for most people,
they're trying

00:32:33.230 --> 00:32:35.600
to just build their applications
to run their businesses.

00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:37.600
And they don't want to
worry about these things.

00:32:37.600 --> 00:32:41.120
And in that case,
non-relational databases

00:32:41.120 --> 00:32:43.070
just don't cut it
for what they need.

00:32:45.740 --> 00:32:48.170
What's the difference between
Cloud Spanner and BigQuery?

00:32:48.170 --> 00:32:51.080
Can't I just use BigQuery?

00:32:51.080 --> 00:32:57.685
So it's basically the difference
between OLTP and OLAP systems.

00:32:57.685 --> 00:32:59.810
So are you looking for an
enterprise data warehouse

00:32:59.810 --> 00:33:02.240
or are you looking for
a operational mission

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:04.670
critical database?

00:33:04.670 --> 00:33:07.120
That is literally when you
would choose one or the other.

00:33:07.120 --> 00:33:11.840
Cloud Spanner for OLTP
systems and BigQuery

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:15.920
for OLAP systems or
enterprise data warehouse.

00:33:15.920 --> 00:33:21.020
So if I were to think about
it in the on-prem world

00:33:21.020 --> 00:33:23.120
or in the traditional
world, it would

00:33:23.120 --> 00:33:28.080
be the difference between
Oracle or SQL Server and Netezza

00:33:28.080 --> 00:33:29.240
or Teradata.

00:33:29.240 --> 00:33:30.950
That's the real difference.

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:32.450
And in fact, we are
basically trying

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:36.470
to make it simple for customers
to use their operational data

00:33:36.470 --> 00:33:39.260
in a data warehouse with
Cloud Spanner or BigQuery

00:33:39.260 --> 00:33:43.310
so you don't have to
have complicated tools

00:33:43.310 --> 00:33:47.096
for ETLing data out of
your relational database,

00:33:47.096 --> 00:33:48.470
out of your
operational database,

00:33:48.470 --> 00:33:50.264
and moving it to
your data warehouse.

00:33:50.264 --> 00:33:51.680
There are simple
tools that we are

00:33:51.680 --> 00:33:55.486
working on that will allow
you to quickly transfer data

00:33:55.486 --> 00:33:56.860
from Cloud Spanner
into BigQuery.

00:33:56.860 --> 00:33:58.490
And then over time
that you don't even

00:33:58.490 --> 00:33:59.690
have to do the ETL at all.

00:33:59.690 --> 00:34:03.110
It can be as simple as
a push button thing.

00:34:03.110 --> 00:34:03.759
Moving on.

00:34:03.759 --> 00:34:05.300
Does Cloud Spanner
have every feature

00:34:05.300 --> 00:34:07.460
that traditional databases do?

00:34:07.460 --> 00:34:09.400
No.

00:34:09.400 --> 00:34:11.969
It's a different
take on the system.

00:34:11.969 --> 00:34:15.989
So there are specific
features that Cloud Spanner

00:34:15.989 --> 00:34:18.560
doesn't have now that we're
working on to fill those gaps.

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:25.110
Like DML, obviously, and
backups, and things like that.

00:34:25.110 --> 00:34:26.550
And triggers and queues.

00:34:26.550 --> 00:34:30.120
These are all part
of our roadmap.

00:34:30.120 --> 00:34:33.120
I would hope that our
audience and our customers

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:36.810
give us some time.

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:40.560
Because we just launched last
year and traditional databases

00:34:40.560 --> 00:34:42.449
have been around
for 30, 40 years.

00:34:42.449 --> 00:34:45.659
So we're trying and
moving very fast.

00:34:45.659 --> 00:34:48.810
And we're going to close those
relational database feature

00:34:48.810 --> 00:34:50.670
gaps that are critical.

00:34:50.670 --> 00:34:52.500
There's certain
things that are not

00:34:52.500 --> 00:34:54.840
critical for relational
databases that

00:34:54.840 --> 00:34:57.240
somehow are supposed to be.

00:34:57.240 --> 00:34:59.190
Stored procedures, for example.

00:34:59.190 --> 00:35:01.830
That's just a way of pushing
down application logic

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:04.860
into the database layer.

00:35:04.860 --> 00:35:09.030
Which we don't actually
think that's the way

00:35:09.030 --> 00:35:11.340
we want to go with our system.

00:35:11.340 --> 00:35:14.910
We want to have a lean,
mean database that

00:35:14.910 --> 00:35:17.280
can do the things you need
it to do, which is you

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:22.770
strong consistency
transaction, et cetera.

00:35:22.770 --> 00:35:25.920
And we want to give this
to you in global scale.

00:35:25.920 --> 00:35:28.834
But we think that application
developers know best how

00:35:28.834 --> 00:35:30.000
to write their applications.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:33.530
And that's the way we want
to offer this service.

00:35:33.530 --> 00:35:36.210
So we the Cloud Spanner, we're
the new take on databases.

00:35:36.210 --> 00:35:41.010
We were able to go back
to first principles

00:35:41.010 --> 00:35:43.020
and think about
why databases exist

00:35:43.020 --> 00:35:45.630
and what they're needed
for, what they're used.

00:35:45.630 --> 00:35:52.529
And we were able to cut
out all the flack, cruft

00:35:52.529 --> 00:35:54.570
that's been building up
in databases for a while.

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:58.380
And we were able to go back to
first principles for databases

00:35:58.380 --> 00:36:04.290
and then provide the features
and functionality that

00:36:04.290 --> 00:36:07.110
are truly core for
databases, especially

00:36:07.110 --> 00:36:08.052
relational databases.

00:36:08.052 --> 00:36:10.510
And as I said, yes, it doesn't
have every feature right now

00:36:10.510 --> 00:36:11.160
which we're going to fill.

00:36:11.160 --> 00:36:13.980
But it also has features that
no other relational database,

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:15.510
or in fact, no
other database has.

00:36:15.510 --> 00:36:18.060
Which is global, strong
consistency, replication out

00:36:18.060 --> 00:36:22.720
of the box, transparent,
securities built in,

00:36:22.720 --> 00:36:26.070
and transparent, et cetera.

00:36:26.070 --> 00:36:27.390
Next question.

00:36:27.390 --> 00:36:32.490
Why is sharding a
database so difficult?

00:36:32.490 --> 00:36:39.300
So if you ever had
to shard a database,

00:36:39.300 --> 00:36:44.220
this would an obvious
answer really.

00:36:44.220 --> 00:36:46.614
So think about a database
with a bunch of tables.

00:36:46.614 --> 00:36:48.780
And now think about the
fact that you can't actually

00:36:48.780 --> 00:36:52.780
buy a big enough box to hold all
that data in all those tables.

00:36:52.780 --> 00:36:56.461
So what you have to do then is,
and I'm going to simplify here.

00:36:56.461 --> 00:36:58.710
There are many things that
go into consideration here.

00:36:58.710 --> 00:37:01.020
But at the simplest
level, what you have to do

00:37:01.020 --> 00:37:04.200
is basically take the table,
if you have 10 tables,

00:37:04.200 --> 00:37:07.440
take each table and then put
it in a different database.

00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:09.420
Because that way
at least the data

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:11.190
set for that table
is going to fit

00:37:11.190 --> 00:37:15.330
in your traditional
single box machine.

00:37:15.330 --> 00:37:19.150
So you have 10 tables across
10 different databases.

00:37:19.150 --> 00:37:22.110
So of course, you can just
keep using your tooling

00:37:22.110 --> 00:37:24.750
and you can keep
using transactions

00:37:24.750 --> 00:37:27.270
all for within that table
or maybe two tables.

00:37:27.270 --> 00:37:29.370
With a table and
index, let's say.

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:33.510
But it behaves exactly the same
as one database for that one

00:37:33.510 --> 00:37:35.220
table and its one index.

00:37:35.220 --> 00:37:38.670
But if you ever had to do
anything across those tables,

00:37:38.670 --> 00:37:41.730
then you have to write
the transaction logic

00:37:41.730 --> 00:37:44.760
in the application
to do the join

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:47.280
and to do the
transactions on them.

00:37:47.280 --> 00:37:51.330
So you essentially will
have to write the protocol

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:53.100
to commit a transaction
that is done

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:55.320
in a database inside
the application layer

00:37:55.320 --> 00:38:00.330
without having the engine
that runs the system.

00:38:00.330 --> 00:38:03.120
The database engine is what runs
these transactions, et cetera,

00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:05.730
and does the consistency
and guarantees, et cetera.

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:09.990
So to do that in the application
layer is extremely hard

00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:11.350
and, as I said, very brittle.

00:38:14.501 --> 00:38:16.500
If you're doing a two-phase
commit, for example,

00:38:16.500 --> 00:38:19.120
what happens if one
shard is unavailable,

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:20.370
but the other shard committed?

00:38:20.370 --> 00:38:22.590
So do you roll back
that shard, or do you

00:38:22.590 --> 00:38:25.120
retry on the first shard?

00:38:25.120 --> 00:38:28.290
And what happens if,
during the commit,

00:38:28.290 --> 00:38:30.310
there was a network outage?

00:38:30.310 --> 00:38:33.630
So do you know whether the
transaction committed or not?

00:38:33.630 --> 00:38:35.910
How do you recover from
that state, et cetera?

00:38:35.910 --> 00:38:37.380
So there's many,
many things that

00:38:37.380 --> 00:38:43.920
go into making sharded
database work well.

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:51.300
And all of them make the
application developers' life

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:53.130
very hard.

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:57.330
So those are some of
the questions we had.

00:38:57.330 --> 00:39:00.090
I don't think we have
any more questions,

00:39:00.090 --> 00:39:03.450
so I think we'll call it a day.

00:39:03.450 --> 00:39:05.910
And thank you,
again, for watching

00:39:05.910 --> 00:39:08.780
and for tuning into
our live webinar.

00:39:08.780 --> 00:39:10.770
As I said, we have
these live webinars

00:39:10.770 --> 00:39:12.310
for Cloud OnAir every Tuesday.

00:39:12.310 --> 00:39:17.460
So please log in next Tuesday
for our upcoming webinars

00:39:17.460 --> 00:39:19.620
on collaboration and
productivity tools,

00:39:19.620 --> 00:39:22.000
as well as machine learning,
among other topics.

00:39:22.000 --> 00:39:22.900
Thank you so much.

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:24.680
Have a great day.

